<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Applying Conditional Statements (Using Lemmas)</title>

    <page>
        
   
            <p>
                Here, we think about taking a conditional statement that we know
                is true, and applying it to some other situation.
                Sometimes this looks like using a lemma in a proof.
                Following is a statement of a theorem which can be proven using
                calculus or precalculus mathematics.
            </p>

        <p>
            A key component of logical reasoning with conditional statements is
            known as <em>modus ponens</em>.
            It works like this:
        </p>

        <p>
            IF
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        you have a conditional statement (of the form <q> if
                        <m>P</m>, then <m>Q</m></q>) that you know is <fillin characters="10" />, AND
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        you can establish that the hypothesis <m>P</m> is <fillin characters="10" />,
                        THEN
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        it necessarily follows that the conclusion <m>Q</m>
                        <fillin characters="10" />.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Use this reasoning in what follows.
        </p>


    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In calculus, we establish that the following conditional
                statement is true:
            </p>


                <blockquote>
                  <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is any function such that <m>f</m> is
                differentiable at the real number <m>x = a</m>, then <m>f</m> is
                continuous at <m>x = a</m>.
                  </p>
                </blockquote>


            <p>
                What can we conclude, if anything about the following four
                functions?
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>g</m> that we know is differentiable at
                    <m>x = 2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>r</m> that we know is not differentiable at
                    <m>x=-5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>h</m> that we know is continuous at
                    <m>x = 3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>h</m> that we know is not continuous at
                    <m>x = -1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

 </page>

 <page>
    


            <theorem xml:id="thm-quadvertex">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f</m> is a quadratic function of the form
                        <m>f(x) = ax^2 +bx + c</m> where
                        <m>a,b,c\in\mathbb{R}</m> and <m>a&lt;0</m>, then the
                        function <m>f</m> has a maximum value when
                        <m>x= \frac{-b}{2a}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </theorem>


        <activity workspace=".5in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Using <em>only</em> <xref ref="thm-quadvertex"/>, what can
                    be concluded about the functions given by the following
                    formulas?
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <p>
                    <m>g(x) = -8x^2 +5x-2</m>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    <m>h(x)=-\frac{1}{3}x^2 + 3x</m>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    <m>k(x) = 8x^2 -5x-7</m>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x) = -4x+2</m>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    <m>j(x) = -\frac{71}{99}x^2 + 210</m>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x) = -x^4 + x^3 + 9</m>
                </p>
            </task>
    
</activity>

 <p>
            There is also <em>modus tollens</em>.
            Which works like this:
        </p>

        <p>
            IF
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        you have a conditional statement (of the form <q> if
                        <m>P</m>, then <m>Q</m></q>) that you know is <fillin characters="10" />, AND
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        you can establish that the conclusion <m>P</m> is <fillin characters="10" />,
                        THEN
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        it necessarily follows that the hypothesis  <m>Q</m> is
                        <fillin characters="10" />>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Can you apply modus tollens to any of the problems in the activity on this page?
        </p>

         </page>




</handout>